[john.russell@canrem.UUCP (John Russell) writes:]
[] There is a major problem with casting spells, and I don't see how it can
[] be fixed without rethinking the rationale behind the magic system.
[] ...
[] A rethink of the whole spell system would make the game more balanced.
[] Go for it!

personally, i would do the following (this exists somewhat now):

1) make spell-magic an integral part of a wizard-characters game.
     this might entail changing the odds of object creation ...
2) limit energy points obtained by increases in experience to
    non-wizard: 1-5 points and wizard: 1-9 points.
3) make spell-learning require Intelligence and experience and
    certain spells would be impossible to learn without unusally
    high degrees of both of these attributes.  spellbooks would
    not normally disappear after being read, so they can be
    re-learned or learned later.
4) make spell-casting require n*n enegy points where n is the
    number associated with the spell.  there would be other
    factors to consider also.  if you learned the spell you can
    probably cast it.
5) introduce a timeout to spell-casting and also remember the level
    of the last spell cast.  during the timeout interval you can
    cast a spell whose level is lower than the last_spell_cast but
    this will extend the timeout and lower the last_spell_cast level.
    the timeout would be non-wizard: 120 * n and for wizards: 80 * n.
6) the number of uses for a spell of level n would be:
    ((max_spell_level - n + 1) * (max_spell_level - n + 1)) + 1
    so if max_spell_level is 7 and n is 1 you would get 50 uses for
    a level 1 spell whereas if n is 7 you would only get 2 uses for
    that spell before being required to relearn the spell (altho
    maybe you could cast it again and pass out/get very hungry/etc.).
    the above formula is for non-wizards, wizards would take the
    number thus obtained and multiply by 3/2.
7) after using a spell, if you only have two uses left then you
    would get a warning.  if you only have 1 use left you would get
    another warning and a timeout of 1 Wisdom point.  if you have no
    uses left you get a reprimand and a timeout of 1 Wisdom point
    and 2 Intelligence points.

this would (i think) make spells a non-trivial feature and make
it an integral part of a wizards game ...

